Author a Crucible fuzz-harness FIXTURE (only) for the Solana program `{{ program }}`.

The fixture is the ONLY thing that can drive the program: the invariant tests written against it may
read state but never send instructions (an invariant that mutates state corrupts the campaign). So
the fixture's `action_*` methods and its initial state decide which properties can be checked at all
— everything you leave out is a property that can only be "checked" by an assertion that is true no
matter what the program does. Design it for these {{ n }} properties:
{{ listed }}

Concretely, for that list:
- **One `action_*` per instruction those properties exercise.** An instruction with no action is
  unreachable state the fuzzer can never explore.
- **Negative attempts are actions too — but the action ATTEMPTS, it never JUDGES.** For a property of
  the form "X must be rejected" (re-init, an unauthorized caller, a flash borrow with no repay, an
  operation while paused), write an action that *attempts* X and **records what happened** in a
  `pub(crate)` field. A rejected call changes nothing, so the attempt is safe here — and the
  assertion belongs to the test that owns the property:

      pub fn action_init_without_signer(&mut self) -> bool {
          let res = self.ctx.program(self.program_id)
              .call(instruction::Initialize {})
              .accounts(accounts::Initialize { /* … */ })
              .signers(&[&*self.intruder])          // NOT the authority
              .send();
          // Record. Do not assert.
          self.accepted.init_without_signer |= res.map(|o| o.is_success()).unwrap_or(false);
          true
      }

  Group the flags in one `#[derive(Clone, Default)] pub(crate) struct Accepted { … }` field so
  `Fixture` stays readable, and give each one the name of the attempt it records.
- **Never assert a property in the fixture.** No `fuzz_assert*` inside an `action_*`, and no
  `"[<property title>] …"` message anywhere in this file. This is a reporting rule, not a style one:
  the fixture is compiled into **every** component's fuzz target, so an assertion here fires in
  campaigns that never asked about the property it names — and the components that do not own that
  title cannot place the counterexample. (Panicking in `setup()` when setup itself fails is fine:
  that is the harness failing, not a property.)
- **A negative action must `return true`.** The bool reports whether the ACTION did what it was
  designed to do — not whether the instruction succeeded — and for this one making the attempt *is*
  it working. Returning `false` costs you twice: Crucible reads `false` as a dead-end state and
  **ends the action sequence there**, so every draw of this action truncates the campaign; and a
  violation reported on a `false` action is auto-labelled a suspected harness bug, which is precisely
  backwards for an action whose entire purpose is the rejection.
- **Enough actors and accounts to make the property non-degenerate.** A property about "only the
  owner may X" needs a second, non-owner signer; "at most N positions" needs enough reserves/markets
  to reach N; a per-user property needs two users. One of each is a fixture that proves nothing.
- **Realistic configuration.** Never set a limit, cap or threshold to `u64::MAX` (or a fee to 0) just
  to keep setup simple: it makes the property about that limit vacuously true. Pick values the fuzzer
  can actually cross.
- **Expose typed reads.** Give the tests helper methods returning the state they must assert on
  (`pub fn reserve(&self) -> State`, a scaled-fraction → integer helper, PDA accessors), so a test
  never has to parse raw account bytes at hand-computed offsets.

State the fixture holds must be reachable from the tests: make the fields the properties talk about
`pub(crate)` or expose an accessor.
